      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "C# / .NET",
      "question": "Interface vs abstract class?",
      "answer": "One-line: An interface defines a contract; an abstract class can share state and implementation.\n\n Interview answer: I use interfaces for loose coupling and capabilities, and abstract classes for closely related types with common behavior.",
      "example": "📜 Contract vs 🏠 parent house\n\n Interface = capability | Abstract = shared base",
