      "topic": "C# / .NET",
      "question": "What happens when you start Thread.Start?",
      "answer": "One-line: It schedules a managed thread to begin executing a method.\n\nInterview answer: I reserve Thread for long-lived or specialized execution. Ordinary .NET application work normally uses Task and ThreadPool instead of manually creating threads.",
      "example": "🔔 New worker enters a job\n\nNew thread → Execute method → Exit when method returns\n\nvar t = new Thread(() => DoWork());\nt.Start();\nt.Join();",
