      "answer": "One-line: Task.Run is preferred because it composes, reports exceptions, and supports cancellation.\n\nInterview answer: I use Task.Run only for CPU-bound work or explicit background work. It is not a solution for blocking or hiding slow I/O.",
      "example": "📮 Raw queue item vs 🎟️ tracked Task result\n\nQueueUserWorkItem = void callback | Task.Run = awaitable result",
