      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "C# / .NET",
      "question": "What causes memory leaks in managed C#?",
      "answer": "One-line: Managed leaks are usually retained references or finite caches without eviction.\n\nInterview answer: I investigate static references, long-lived caches, event subscriptions, incomplete disposals, timers, pending Tasks, and large retained objects with heap snapshots and metrics.",
      "example": "📌 Still-reachable objects are never free\n\nStatic/cache/Event/subscription/continuation → retained object graph",
