      "question": "How does dependency injection create an endpoint's services?",
      "answer": "At startup, registrations describe service-to-implementation mappings and lifetimes. When an endpoint needs IOrderService, the built-in container follows its constructor dependencies and creates the required object graph. Singleton instances are shared by the root provider; scoped instances are reused inside one scope (for web requests, normally one request scope); transient instances are created for each resolution. These are lifetime rules, not thread-safety guarantees.\n\n The request scope also tracks disposable services it creates and disposes them when that scope ends. A singleton must not capture a request-scoped service such as a DbContext; doing so leaks the wrong lifetime across requests. Creating a scope for background work is explicit—the request scope does not follow a queued job after the response ends.\n\n Check the design: Is the service safe to share? Does its dependency have a longer or shorter lifetime? Who disposes it? DI can construct objects, but it cannot make mutable singleton state thread-safe.",
      "example": "🧰 The container follows a constructor graph and owns scoped cleanup\n\n Endpoint asks for service → constructor dependencies resolved → scope cache checked → instance supplied → scope disposed\n\nbuilder.Services.AddDbContext<OrdersDbContext>(options =>\n    options.UseSqlServer(connectionString)); // scoped by default\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();\nbuilder.Services.AddSingleton<IOrderRules, OrderRules>();",
