      "question": "Topic 2 · Sagas and the transactional outbox: recover across independent systems",
      "answer": "Why it matters: a SQL transaction cannot atomically commit both a database row and a remote payment-provider action. Holding a database transaction open during a network call ties up locks and connections, yet a crash can still occur after the provider acts and before your app records the result.\n\nHow to build it: each step is a local transaction with a durable state transition. The saga is a workflow, not a magic distributed rollback. A compensation (such as releasing a reservation) is a new business action; it may itself fail and must be retryable. Define which states are terminal, how late payment is handled, and how an operator can repair a stuck workflow.\n\n SQL shape · save business intent and event together\nThe relay may publish and crash before marking the row delivered, so it can publish again. Consumers must deduplicate with a durable event/business key and commit their deduplication record with their own side effect. “Exactly once” is achieved as an idempotent business effect, not assumed from transport delivery.\n\nObserved outcome | Persisted state | Safe next action |\n\nProvider confirms success | Payment = Succeeded; order transition is conditional | Emit OrderConfirmed once; fulfill idempotently |\n\nProvider confirms decline | Payment = Declined; order remains unconfirmed | Release reservation through an idempotent compensation |\n\nRequest times out / response unknown | Payment = Unknown or Pending | Query by provider key/reference or reconcile callback; do not send a new logical charge |\n\nDuplicate/out-of-order callback | Keep valid monotonic state transition and event ID | Deduplicate and ignore stale transition; alert on impossible state |\n\nTest: crash after DB commit, after publish, after provider success, and before local result commit. Re-run each operation and prove there is still one order, one logical charge, and a recoverable state. See reliability and distributed primitives.",
      "example": "1 · Order DBCommit Pending order + PaymentRequested outbox row\n\n→\n\n2 · Outbox relayPublish durable message; mark delivered\n\n→\n\n3 · Payment workerCall provider with stable idempotency key\n\n→\n\n4 · Result ownerRecord outcome + next outbox event\n\nFailure path: unknown provider result → keep order/payment Pending → query provider or consume signed callback → reconcile → confirm or release reservation according to policy. Never guess “failed” from a timeout.\n\nBEGIN;\n\nINSERT INTO orders (order_id, status, idempotency_key)\nVALUES (:order_id, 'PENDING_PAYMENT', :request_key);\n\nINSERT INTO outbox (event_id, aggregate_id, event_type, payload)\nVALUES (:event_id, :order_id, 'PaymentRequested', :payload);\n\nCOMMIT;",
      "practice_type": "Concept review",
