      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "Identity & Security",
      "concept": "Token validation versus decoding",
      "question": "Why does reading claims from a decoded JWT not prove that the caller is authenticated?",
      "answer": "A JWT payload is an encoded claim set, not evidence that the token is trustworthy. Validate its signature using trusted configuration, expected issuer and audience, allowed algorithms, lifetime, and relevant application rules. Then authorize the requested operation. Do not accept client-supplied roles or a decoded claim as a substitute for verification.",
      "example": "Untrusted token -> standard validation middleware -> verified principal.\nVerified principal + resource policy -> authorization decision.\nDo not grant Pro because an unverified payload says tier=Pro.",
