      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "JavaScript / TypeScript",
      "concept": "Static types versus runtime input",
      "question": "Why does casting an API response to a TypeScript interface not validate its data?",
      "answer": "Type assertions affect compile-time checking and are erased from the emitted JavaScript. External JSON can still be malformed or malicious. Validate unknown input with an appropriate runtime schema or explicit guards before relying on its fields. Keep that validation at the boundary and distinguish a missing value from a value that violates a business rule.",
      "example": "const value: unknown = await response.json();\nif (!isProduct(value)) throw new Error(\"Invalid product response\");\n// isProduct must actually inspect the required runtime fields.",
