      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "JavaScript / TypeScript",
      "concept": "Discriminated unions",
      "question": "How can a discriminated union prevent reading a result before a request succeeds?",
      "answer": "Model each state as its own variant with a shared discriminant. Narrowing on that field makes the success payload available only in the success branch, instead of marking every field optional and hoping callers remember the rules. Runtime input still needs validation. An exhaustive check helps reveal missing cases when the state model evolves.",
      "example": "type Result<T> = { state: \"loading\" } | { state: \"success\"; data: T } | { state: \"error\"; message: string };\nfunction text(r: Result<string>) {\n  return r.state === \"success\" ? r.data : r.state;\n}",
