      "id": "kr7-interview-90d9000687577959196a18e8",
      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "JavaScript / TypeScript",
      "concept": "Event loop and microtasks",
      "question": "Why can a resolved Promise callback run before a setTimeout callback scheduled first?",
      "answer": "Promise reactions run as microtasks, while a timer schedules a later task after its delay threshold and event-loop scheduling allow it. After the current synchronous work completes, queued microtasks are processed before moving to the next task. A zero-millisecond timeout is not an immediate call or a precise deadline. Excessive microtasks can delay rendering and other tasks.",
      "example": "setTimeout(() => console.log(\"timer\"), 0);\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"promise\"));\nconsole.log(\"sync\");\n// sync, promise, timer",
