      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "Data Structures & Algorithms",
      "concept": "Sliding window assumptions",
      "question": "Why can a two-pointer sum window fail when an array includes negative numbers?",
      "answer": "For nonnegative inputs, expanding a sum window cannot decrease its sum, enabling monotonic pointer decisions for some problems. Negative values break that property, so shrinking or expanding based only on the current sum can skip valid answers. Use an approach matching the exact problem, such as prefix sums with a hash map for counting subarrays with a target sum.",
      "example": "For [2, -1, 2], extending a window can reduce or increase the sum.\nFor exact target counts, record prefix frequencies and look up prefixSum - target.",
      "practice_type": "Original interview Q&A and concept review"
