      "id": "kr7-interview-2fe7f73410b4498fcffd4d40",
      "company": "Subject practice",
      "topic": "Data Structures & Algorithms",
      "concept": "BFS versus DFS",
      "question": "Why does breadth-first search find a shortest edge-count path in an unweighted graph?",
      "answer": "BFS explores vertices in nondecreasing distance from the start using a queue. Marking a vertex when enqueued prevents repeated discovery and preserves the first distance. DFS does not generally discover the shortest route first. For weighted graphs, use an algorithm whose assumptions match the edge weights rather than applying ordinary BFS indiscriminately.",
      "example": "Queue holds (vertex, distance).\nStart is visited at distance 0.\nEach unseen neighbor is enqueued at distance + 1.\nRecord parents if the actual path is required.",
      "practice_type": "Original interview Q&A and concept review"
