      "concept": "Backtracking",
      "question": "What distinguishes backtracking from greedily committing to the first locally attractive choice?",
      "answer": "Backtracking explores candidates and undoes choices to examine alternatives, pruning states that cannot lead to a solution. Greedy methods commit based on a property that must be justified for the problem. Explain search size and pruning rather than claiming recursion makes exponential work efficient.",
